 "title": "Powers and Roots",
 "summary": "Learn to read small raised numbers, find missing starting numbers, and pick special number types from lists.",
 "blocks": [
  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "## Squares"
  },
  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "To square a number we multiply the number by itself.  \n3 squared is 9 because 3 × 3 = 9  \n4 squared is 16 because 4 × 4 = 16"
  },
  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "We can write squared using a small (superscript) 2.  \n5² means 5 squared  \n10² means 10 squared"
  },
  {
   "kind": "worked-example",
   "title": "Example 1: Work out the value of 9²",
   "steps": [
    {
     "heading": null,
     "body": "To work out the value of 9 squared we need to calculate 9 × 9  \n9 × 9 = 81  \n9² = 81",
     "figure_refs": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "kind": "worked-example",
   "title": "Example 2: Work out the value of (-8)²",
   "steps": [
    {
     "heading": null,
     "body": "To work out the value of -8 squared we need to calculate -8 × -8  \n\\-8 × -8 = 64 (A negative times a negative is a positive)  \n(-8)² = 64  \nIf you are using a calculator you must put the -8 in brackets, otherwise the calculator will get the answer wrong!",
     "figure_refs": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
